<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="dolabralexins biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>Diterpenoids are a large class of more than 10,000 known compounds, with diverse physiological and biological functions. Plants typically harbor a widely conserved subset of diterpenoids with diverse roles in chemical ecology and plant adaptation. Two groups of terpenoids, the diterpenoid |FRAME: Kauralexins kauralexins| and the sesquiterpenoid |FRAME: Zealexins zealexins|, are well described from maize (although their biosynthesis is not well understood). These compounds participate in defense against pathogenic fungi, both above and below-ground. The kauralexins also function as insect feeding deterrents, and accumulate in response to salt and drought stress. This pathway describes the biosynthesis of a different branch of diterpenoids known as dolabralexins Dolabralexins biosynthesis is induced by oxidative stress and elicitation with the fungal pathogens |FRAME: TAX-117187| and |FRAME: TAX-5518|. |FRAME: CPD-21266 &quot;15,16-Epoxydolabrene&quot;| significantly inhibited the growth of both pathogens &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt;, while |FRAME: CPD-21268| inhibition was limited to |FRAME: TAX-117187| |CITS: [29475898]|. The core compound of this branch, |FRAME: CPD-21265|, is formed from the common precursor |FRAME: GERANYLGERANYL-PP| by the action of two diterpene synthases - |FRAME: EC-5.5.1.13| (|FRAME: G-14667|) and |FRAME: EC-4.2.3.196| (|FRAME: G-58304|). This compound is then converted into |FRAME: CPD-21266| and its hydroxylated form |FRAME: CPD-21267| by the cytochrome P-450 |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.159| (|FRAME: G-58305|). Hydrolysis of the epoxy group of |FRAME: CPD-21267| leads to formation of a tri-hydroxylated form, which accumulates as the dominant dolabralexin pathway metabolite. It is not known whether |FRAME: CPD-21268| is formed via an enzymatic or spontaneous hydrolysis |CITS: [29475898]|. The production of dolabralexin is inducible. Increased transcript levels of all three genes was observed upon exposure to CuSO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;, with |FRAME: G-14667| showing the most significant up-regulation of ~400-fold. Similarly, accumulation of |FRAME: CPD-21265|, |FRAME: CPD-21266|, and |FRAME: CPD-21268| was induced significantly by inoculation with live Fusarium spores.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="chawkins" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
